WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.759
Temporary file security must cover the whole pipeline, not just the file
creation call.

2
00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:19.358
Upload processing, image conversion, document rendering, archive
extraction, report generation, batch jobs, file handoff between services,
cloud storage staging, and subprocess workflows all create opportunities
for temporary data to move between components.

3
00:00:19.358 --> 00:00:25.211
A safe first file does not guarantee a safe pipeline if later steps copy,
rename, log, or expose the artifact.

4
00:00:25.211 --> 00:00:33.186
Upload and archive workflows are especially sensitive because the
application receives content and metadata from outside the trusted
boundary.

5
00:00:33.186 --> 00:00:45.148
Temporary staging should use application-controlled locations and names,
enforce size and type expectations, and keep extracted or transformed
content isolated until it is validated and authorized for the next step.

6
00:00:45.148 --> 00:00:53.444
Archive handling should validate entries and extraction destinations
conceptually without relying on user-supplied paths to define storage
layout.

7
00:00:53.444 --> 00:00:55.824
Subprocess integration also needs review.

8
00:00:55.824 --> 00:01:02.834
A child process may read a temporary input file, write an output file,
generate diagnostic text, or leave partial results behind.

9
00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:09.523
The parent Python code should control the paths, working directory,
environment, timeout, output handling, and cleanup.

10
00:01:09.523 --> 00:01:17.562
The subprocess should receive only the files it needs, and logs should
avoid exposing private file contents or sensitive paths from command
output.

11
00:01:17.562 --> 00:01:22.578
Transactions and rollback behavior matter when temporary files support
business actions.

12
00:01:22.578 --> 00:01:30.231
If a report generation job fails halfway through, the application should
know whether partial output exists and whether it is safe to delete.

13
00:01:30.231 --> 00:01:36.277
If an upload succeeds but metadata storage fails, the temporary file
should not remain orphaned indefinitely.

14
00:01:36.277 --> 00:01:43.480
Pipelines need cleanup after success, failure, cancellation, and retry so
temporary artifacts do not become permanent surprises.
